\description{
The design twin of \code{\link[=table_battery]{table_battery()}}: a battery of items answered on
one common scale, one row per item and one column per level, with row
percentages estimated from a \code{survey::svydesign()} or
\code{survey::as.svrepdesign()} object instead of a data frame.

Every statistic is survey's. \code{survey::svymean()} estimates the
percentages of each item, and, with \code{by}, \code{survey::svychisq()} tests
each item against the group, Rao-Scott corrected by default, the
p-values adjusted for the family of the items by Holm's method.

The rows, the columns, \code{levels}, \code{top}, \code{sort} and the outputs are
those of \code{\link[=table_battery]{table_battery()}}.
}
\section{Estimation}{
The percentage of a level is \code{survey::svymean()} of the item on its
domain: within the group under \code{by}, the rows of non-zero weight whose
answer is a level shown. The domain is cut from the design with \code{[},
which keeps its strata, clusters and finite population corrections.
Under \code{drop_na = FALSE}, the missing answers are a level of their own
and stay in the domain. An answer that \code{levels} leaves out is outside
the domain either way. An item that offers one level is 100\% by
arithmetic: survey does not estimate a variable with one level.

A cell that survey cannot estimate, most often because a stratum holds
a single primary sampling unit under its default
\code{options(survey.lonely.psu = "fail")}, shows a dash: the function
warns once, quoting survey's messages (class
\code{spicy_svy_estimation_failed}), and the note says what the dash means.

\code{n} is the observed count of the domain, the respondents and not the
estimated population, as in \code{\link[=table_categorical_svy]{table_categorical_svy()}}. A row with a
missing \code{by} leaves the table, and the note gives their count.
}

\section{Tests}{
With \code{by}, each item is tested against the group by
\code{survey::svychisq()} with \code{chisq_statistic}, on its answers on the
levels shown, the missing answers left out whatever \code{drop_na}; the
levels and the groups without an answer leave the test, as in
\code{\link[=table_battery]{table_battery()}}. The default \code{"F"} is the Pearson chi-squared with
the Rao-Scott second-order correction, referred to the design degrees
of freedom. The p-values are adjusted for the family of the items, by
Holm's method by default (\code{p_adjust}). An item left with one level is
not tested: its \code{p} cell shows a dash, and the note names it.

A design whose weights go negative, as linear calibration can make
them, keeps its percentages, and its tests are withheld, with a
warning (class \code{spicy_negative_weights_no_test}), as in
\code{\link[=table_categorical_svy]{table_categorical_svy()}}.
}

\section{What is absent}{
No interval or design effect per cell: a battery of five levels in
three groups would carry thirty of each, and
\code{\link[=table_categorical_svy]{table_categorical_svy()}} gives them item by item. No column
percentages, and no mean of the scale.
}

